From c8a4c3a357d5c1fdb4d1b0f6cad590a04877e4e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <pkgdemonteam@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:31:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] NextBSD: evdev: end driver frames with SYN_REPORT 0, as Linux
 does

evdev_sync() ended every driver-initiated frame with SYN_REPORT value
1. On Linux, input_sync() sends 0. The input core sends 1 only for a
frame it inserts on its own, such as after a key auto-repeat event.

libinput 1.29.0 and later rely on that difference: the touchpad
dispatch (tp_interface_process) drops every frame whose SYN_REPORT
value is 1 as a kernel-inserted frame (libinput issue #1145). With
evdev_sync() sending 1, that is every frame from every touchpad
driver: psm (Synaptics, Elantech), hmt, wsp, bcm5974. Touch, motion
and the touchpad's own button clicks are all lost, while mice,
TrackPoints and keyboards, which go through the fallback dispatch,
keep working.

Send 0 from evdev_sync(). Keep 1 at the two places where the evdev
core inserts a frame itself, which is exactly the Linux meaning:
evdev_repeat_callout() (key auto-repeat) and
evdev_restore_after_kdb() (the stuck-key release after kdb).

Audit of sys/ on releng/15.1: no driver pushes SYN_REPORT directly;
drivers only declare EV_SYN support and end frames through
evdev_sync(). uinput passes through whatever userland writes. Inside
the kernel, evdev_sparse_event(), evdev_propagate_event() and the
cdev client queue all test code == SYN_REPORT and never the value,
so nothing changes in the kernel itself.

Refs nextbsd/nextbsd-kernel#344. Carried out-of-tree for NextBSD;
the same change is suitable for FreeBSD, where stock FreeBSD with
libinput 1.29 or later has the same problem.
---
 sys/dev/evdev/evdev.h | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sys/dev/evdev/evdev.h b/sys/dev/evdev/evdev.h
index 6d73aa7d..7bfcd4bf 100644
--- a/sys/dev/evdev/evdev.h
+++ b/sys/dev/evdev/evdev.h
@@ -189,11 +189,18 @@ void evdev_support_nfingers(struct evdev_dev *, int);
 void evdev_push_nfingers(struct evdev_dev *, int);
 
 /* Event reporting shortcuts: */
+
+/*
+ * A driver-initiated frame ends with SYN_REPORT 0, as Linux's input_sync()
+ * does. SYN_REPORT 1 means a frame the input core inserted on its own (key
+ * auto-repeat, the key release after kdb), and libinput's touchpad code drops
+ * those frames.
+ */
 static __inline int
 evdev_sync(struct evdev_dev *evdev)
 {
 
-	return (evdev_push_event(evdev, EV_SYN, SYN_REPORT, 1));
+	return (evdev_push_event(evdev, EV_SYN, SYN_REPORT, 0));
 }
 
 static __inline int
